SOURCE corpus/knowledge/mutation-case-kimi-census.md sha256=034524eff8f19cb8fca78cd5d13c6da9971f2ef2b5bedc1b950309c95c121548
# Mutation case — kumitate `kimi-census.mjs` (TAMESHI-R2-1)

Run date 2026-10-14. Lane: tameshi (round 2; plan `tameshi-shokunin/plans/round-2.plan.md`,
axis: depth). Substrate: kumitate-shokunin **read-only** — every mutant is a copy under
`/tmp/tameshi-r2-1-work/`, and no fleet file was written. No live `~/.kimi-code` path was
read: every run is `--selftest` (fixtures under the suite's own `mkdtemp` root) or `--root`
pointed at an empty dir under `/tmp`.

Anchors: **O** = observation (file:line or command output), **I** = inference, **U** =
unknown. A bare `kimi-census.mjs:NNN` is `kumitate-shokunin/scripts/kimi-census.mjs` at the
revision pinned below.

Artifact under test (O: `sha256sum`, `stat`, `git rev-parse`): sha256
`83016d45049b009a6c9868dc392ff6ebcecb357c9abfe423f476459f2724f426`, 896 lines, 43273 B,
mtime 2026-10-13 18:53:12; kumitate repo `175de6ba2e9e904010411b19cab27e0af9aad3d4`,
`git status --porcelain -- scripts/kimi-census.mjs` empty.

## 0. Method and headline

- O: control run, unmutated copy — `node pristine.mjs --selftest` →
  `KIMI_CENSUS_SELFTEST: 43/43 pass` / `VERDICT: PASS`, exit 0 (node v24.21.0).
- O: each defect = ONE exact-string replacement applied to a fresh copy of the pristine
  copy; the driver asserts the old string occurs exactly once before writing
  (`mutate_driver.py --check` → `all unique`, first-line match on all 10). No defect
  compounds with another.
- O: per-defect evidence: `node mutant-<id>.mjs --selftest` full stdout →
  `evidence/<id>.selftest.txt`; exit code + mutant sha256 → `evidence/<id>.meta.json`;
  aggregate → `evidence/results.json`.
- O: **kill rate 8/10 (80 %)**; survivors D2, D10. No inconclusive run — each of the
  11 runs (control + 10) produced a definitive suite verdict; the whole sweep took 7.0 s
  wall against the 300 s timeout.
- O: suite count held: every mutant run still enumerated the full suite (`<n>/43`), and
  all three green runs printed exactly `KIMI_CENSUS_SELFTEST: 43/43 pass`.
- O: restore proof — original sha256 before `83016d45…4f426` and after `83016d45…4f426`;
  size 43273 B and mtime unchanged; kumitate working tree clean for that path.
- Context: the corpus carries no kill-rate measurement on any gate yet
  (`corpus/README.md:7` records that gap); this note is tameshi's first measured one.

## 1. Kill-rate table

| # | Defect class | Injected defect (ref = edit site) | Exit | Suite line | Verdict | Failing cases |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| — | control | unmutated copy | 0 | `43/43 pass` / `VERDICT: PASS` | green | 0 |
| D1 | off-by-one count | K2 counted dir `counted: 18` → `17` (kimi-census.mjs:105) | 1 | `27/43 pass` / `FAIL` | **killed** | 16 (incl. the exact baseline) |
| D2 | off-by-one count (findings cap) | `findings.slice(0, 6)` → `slice(0, 5)` (kimi-census.mjs:378) | 0 | `43/43 pass` / `PASS` | **survived** | 0 |
| D3 | inverted condition | `if (!e.children.includes(n))` → `if (e.children.includes(n))` (kimi-census.mjs:332) | 1 | `27/43 pass` / `FAIL` | **killed** | 16 |
| D4 | dropped arm | root-stray reporting loop deleted (kimi-census.mjs:351-353) | 1 | `40/43 pass` / `FAIL` | **killed** | 3 |
| D5 | dropped arm (presence gate) | chain presence `gens.length > 0` → `true` (kimi-census.mjs:255) | 1 | `42/43 pass` / `FAIL` | **killed** | 1 |
| D6 | wrong mode pin | `server.token` floor `0o600` → `0o644` (kimi-census.mjs:124) | 1 | `41/43 pass` / `FAIL` | **killed** | 2 |
| D7 | weakened predicate (floor semantics) | `(mode & floor) !== floor` → `(mode & floor) === 0` (kimi-census.mjs:306) | 1 | `41/43 pass` / `FAIL` | **killed** | 2 |
| D8 | wrong exit-code mapping | `failClosed` `return 2` → `return 1` (kimi-census.mjs:396) | 1 | `40/43 pass` / `FAIL` | **killed** | 3 |
| D9 | weakened secrecy pin | secret kind pin `isLeaf(kind)` → `kind === 'file'` (kimi-census.mjs:292) | 1 | `41/43 pass` / `FAIL` | **killed** | 2 |
| D10 | wrong reported constant | `FROZEN.K2 25` → `24` (kimi-census.mjs:145) | 0 | `43/43 pass` / `PASS` | **survived** | 0 |

By class: off-by-one 1/2 · inverted condition 1/1 · dropped arm 2/2 · wrong mode pin 1/1 ·
weakened predicate 1/1 · wrong exit mapping 1/1 · weakened secrecy pin 1/1 · wrong
reported constant 0/1.

Failing-case labels of the narrow kills (O: `evidence/<id>.selftest.txt`):

- D4 (3): `UNLISTED: a planted undeclared root child …`; `UNLISTED: a hidden root child is
  gated too …`; `root enum is orthogonal to counted-dir drift …` — exactly the three
  root-enumeration cases; no other case moves.
- D5 (1): `MEMBER_MISSING: the drift chain vanished entirely (presence is gated)` — a
  single-case tripwire.
- D6 (2): `SHAPE_DRIFT: server.token below its 0600 floor (checked counts the failure)`;
  `secrecy: server.token is a FIFO → still CLEAN …` (collateral, see §3).
- D7 (2): `SHAPE_DRIFT: credentials/ below its 0700 floor`; `SHAPE_DRIFT: server.token
  below its 0600 floor …`.
- D8 (3): the three fail-closed cases (`--root /nonexistent`, unreadable root, `--json`
  fail-closed).
- D9 (2): `secrecy: server.token is a FIFO → still CLEAN …`; `secrecy: a dangling symlink
  token is never followed (lstat-only → CLEAN)`.
- D1/D3 (16 each): the exact green baseline (`green fixture → CLEAN exit 0, members=49
  checked=49 chain=3 info=4`), the two `--json` family/totals cases, and 13 more — the
  remaining CLEAN/exact-line cases plus the count-string (`'19 children, 18 declared'`)
  and no-UNLISTED asserts; each fails because the green fixture itself now drifts. Full
  lists in `evidence/results.json`.

## 2. Surviving-defect class analysis

**D2 — render truncation off-by-one (kimi-census.mjs:378).** O: 43/43 PASS.
O: the boundary is never exercised — a probe copy (pristine + `appendFileSync` of
`findings.length` at `renderVerdict` entry; output unchanged, still 43/43) logged all 28
text-mode verify runs the suite makes: findings-count distribution `0×8, 1×19, 2×1`,
**max = 2**. O: the DRIFT verdict is asserted only by prefix —
`lastLine(run).startsWith('KIMI_CENSUS: DRIFT')` (kimi-census.mjs:498) plus substrings;
the only exact verdict-line assert is the CLEAN line (`lastLine(r) === 'KIMI_CENSUS:
CLEAN (members=49 checked=49 chain=3 info=4)'`, kimi-census.mjs:511-512).
O (non-equivalence): on an empty root (28 findings) — pristine prints six ids then
`+22 more`; D2 prints five ids then `+23 more`; both exit 1. The mutant is observably
different on ≥6-finding input and the suite cannot see it.

**D10 — wrong reported constant (kimi-census.mjs:145).** O: 43/43 PASS.
O: the only assert touching the frozen block is `j.totals.frozen.K5 === 3`
(kimi-census.mjs:526); the text `FAMILY … frozen=${FROZEN[f]}` line
(kimi-census.mjs:415) and `totals.frozen` (kimi-census.mjs:365) carry no assert anywhere.
O (non-equivalence): same empty root — pristine `FAMILY K2  members=0  checked=0
frozen=25`; D10 `… frozen=24`.

**Shared class: reported-but-unasserted surfaces.** Both survivors change *displayed
report* data and nothing the suite's asserts read. D2 lives in the verdict line's tail
past the asserted prefix; D10 lives in a constant only *reported* (the header itself calls
FROZEN an aid "for callers that compare to round 19/20", kimi-census.mjs:144-145).
I: the suite's assert vocabulary (exit code + prefix + substrings + selected counts)
pins behavior, not reporting completeness — a mutant confined to reporting survives by
construction. Whether other gates share this class is unmeasured (U4).

## 3. What the 43-case suite protects — and does not

**Protected (each proven by the kill that died on it):**

- Declared-count ↔ live-count agreement, both directions: D1 (a single-table edit, which
  desyncs `MEMBERS.counted` :105 from the duplicate `ENUMERABLE.childCount` :137) dies on
  16 cases including the exact baseline.
- The UNLISTED child predicate: D3 dies on 16 cases — the inversion flags every declared
  child of a green fixture, so the baseline itself catches it.
- The root-enumeration arm, including hidden children: D4 dies on exactly the three
  root-enum cases (arm isolation: deleting the reporting loop moves no other case).
- Drift-chain presence: D5 dies on the single no-chain case — thin, but a working tripwire
  for this defect.
- The fail-closed exit map: D8 dies on exactly the three fail-closed cases.
- Mode-floor value and checked accounting: D6 dies on the two loose-mode cases; the FIFO
  case falls as collateral (O: `fixtureTree` chmods members to `m.modeFloor ?? …`
  :437/:441, and the FIFO is made at fixed `-m 600` :637, so the mutant floor also fails a
  clean fixture path).
- Floor *semantics*: D7 dies on the two loose-mode cases. O: under the mutant both loose
  fixtures report `KIMI_CENSUS: CLEAN (members=49 checked=49 …)`, exit 0 — i.e. the
  fixtures use partial-overlap modes (0o500 vs 0o700 floor; 0o400 vs 0o600 floor) that
  discriminate "contains the floor" from "intersects the floor". I: a suite that tested
  only modes sharing **no** floor bits (e.g. 0o000) would have missed this class.
- The secret inode-kind pin: D9 dies on exactly the two anti-read fixtures (FIFO token,
  dangling symlink) — the secrecy cases double as leaf-pin guards.

**Not protected (measured gaps):**

- The verdict-line truncation boundary and its `+N more` arithmetic (D2): max findings in
  any suite run = 2 against a cap of 6. Because the DRIFT asserts are prefix-based (:498),
  even a case reaching 6 findings would not pin the tail.
- Frozen-block constants other than K5 (D10): only `frozen.K5` is asserted (:526).
- (I) Both gaps sit in the same region: reporting surfaces whose values no case reads.
  A caller consuming `--json` or the FAMILY lines is protected by nothing here.

**Scope (I):** this experiment mutates one artifact with single-text-edit defects. It does
not measure the live verify path against substrate drift (the suite's complement), and it
says nothing about coordinated multi-edit rewrites (U1).

## 4. Reproduce

Scratch (all under `/tmp/tameshi-r2-1-work/`): `pristine.mjs` (byte-copy of the pinned
artifact), `mutant-D{1..10}.mjs`, `probe.mjs`, `mutate_driver.py`, `evidence/`,
`empty-root/`. Driver spec = the ten exact edits, shown here so the run can be rebuilt
from the note alone (each applied to a fresh copy):

```
D1  kimi-census.mjs:105   kind: 'dir', counted: 18 },                  -> kind: 'dir', counted: 17 },
D2  kimi-census.mjs:378   findings.slice(0, 6).map                    -> findings.slice(0, 5).map
D3  kimi-census.mjs:332     if (!e.children.includes(n)) {            ->   if (e.children.includes(n)) {
D4  kimi-census.mjs:351-353   the three-line `for (const n of strays) { err(...) }` block deleted
D5  kimi-census.mjs:255   const present = gens.length > 0;            -> const present = true;
D6  kimi-census.mjs:124   kind: 'file', secret: true, modeFloor: 0o600 }, -> …0o644 },
D7  kimi-census.mjs:306   (mode & m.modeFloor) !== m.modeFloor        -> (mode & m.modeFloor) === 0
D8  kimi-census.mjs:396   return 2;                                   -> return 1;
D9  kimi-census.mjs:292   ? (… : isLeaf(kind))                        -> ? (… : kind === 'file')
D10 kimi-census.mjs:145   FROZEN K2: 25                               -> K2: 24
```

Commands (O: run in this order):

```
rm -rf /tmp/tameshi-r2-1-work && mkdir -p /tmp/tameshi-r2-1-work/evidence
cp -p kumitate-shokunin/scripts/kimi-census.mjs /tmp/tameshi-r2-1-work/pristine.mjs
sha256sum /tmp/tameshi-r2-1-work/pristine.mjs          # 83016d45…4f426
python3 /tmp/tameshi-r2-1-work/mutate_driver.py --check
python3 /tmp/tameshi-r2-1-work/mutate_driver.py --run  # control + 10 mutants
node /tmp/tameshi-r2-1-work/mutant-D2.mjs  --root /tmp/tameshi-r2-1-work/empty-root | tail -1
node /tmp/tameshi-r2-1-work/mutant-D10.mjs --root /tmp/tameshi-r2-1-work/empty-root | grep 'FAMILY K2'
```

Sensitivity check expected from the note alone: D2's verdict line must show one fewer id
and `+23 more`; D10's FAMILY K2 line must read `frozen=24`.

## 5. Calibration and errata (append-only)

E1 — the author's pre-run expectation held for 9/10 defects; it was **wrong on D7**. The
expectation filed D7 as a survivor (reasoning: "the loose fixtures lack the floor bits
entirely, so both predicates fire"). Measured: both loose fixtures go CLEAN under D7 and
the mutant dies on the two mode cases. The error was in the *reasoning about the fixture
modes*, not the edit: credentials 0o500 vs floor 0o700 and server.token 0o400 vs floor
0o600 share bits with their floors, so `(mode & floor) === 0` admits them while
`!== floor` rejects them (§3, D7). Recorded as a calibration miss.

## 6. Unknowns

- U1 — a *coordinated* edit of both count tables (`MEMBERS.counted` :105 **and**
  `ENUMERABLE.childCount` :137) was not probed. (I: the literal-count asserts
  `'19 children, 18 declared'` (:577, :730) would still kill it; not measured.)
- U2 — only single-text-edit defects. Multi-site semantic rewrites, check reordering,
  and timing/timeout mutants are outside this experiment.
- U3 — the max-findings probe covers text-mode verify runs only (`renderVerdict` path);
  `--json` and fail-closed runs are not in the distribution.
- U4 — survivor-class generality: one artifact. Whether other gates' suites share the
  "reported-but-unasserted" gap is unmeasured.
- U5 — kill-rate stability across node versions/hosts: measured only on node v24.21.0,
  this host, 2026-10-14.
- U6 — nothing here measures the suite against *substrate* drift; a hermetic suite never
  reads the live tree by construction.## Errata (round 5) — VER-T1 cross-check landing (append-only)

Applied 2026-10-14 by lane T5-2; scope: this file only; nothing above this block
is edited. Source: **VER-T1**, the round-4 independent cross-check of the
arm-class matrix's row E ("Unreached arms"), against the pinned bytes of
`kumitate-shokunin/scripts/kimi-census.mjs` @ sha256 `83016d45…4f426` (scratch
`/tmp/w4h-ver-t1-mut/`; cited in the round-4 `LOG.md` entry). Entry **E2**.

E2 (precision — "unreached" wording scope for this JS case). VER-T1 re-cut the
two JS arm-deletion mutants independently (D4 → 40/43 on exactly the three
root-enum labels; D5 → 42/43 on the singleton chain label — both identical to
§1), and re-measured the D2 boundary with its own probe: 28 text-mode verify
runs, findings distribution `0×8, 1×19, 2×1`, max **2** vs the cap of 6
(`:378`; the cap and the prefix-based DRIFT assert `:498` re-read live by T5-2
on the pinned sha). Two readings the numbers support must be kept apart:

1. the ≥6-findings truncation region is **fixture-unreached** — no suite run
   reaches the boundary, so the region is absent from the fixture set, not
   merely under-asserted; and
2. D2's survival is **assert-caused, not unreachability-caused** — the DRIFT
   assert is prefix-based (`:498`), so even a 6-finding fixture would not pin
   the `+N more` tail. D2 remains a reported-but-unasserted survivor (matrix
   rows A/D), **not** an instance of the unreached-arm class; that class's sole
   instance among the 20 mutants is bash B5.

Wording consequence: matrix §6's literal "Nothing in the JS case is an arm no
fixture reaches" overstates as written — any such generalization must be scoped
to the probed arm-deletion mutants (D4/D5). Cited here because the JS case's
evidence originates in this note; this is a scope note only — no verdict, cell,
or count in §0-§6 above changes, and §5's E1 stands. Companion VER-T1 erratum
(chmod enumeration `:608`/`:654`) lands in `knowledge/arm-class-matrix.md`
(E3 there).

SOURCE tools/mutate-and-run.sh sha256=d22c4025e8ce38b8890bedc1eb9eb5f61366c22f4741910bbe5a942582294a8b
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# mutate-and-run.sh — generic mutation runner (tameshi-shokunin, r10-2, TAMESHI-T10-2).
#
# Channels: r3-2 (TAMESHI-R3-2) shipped the runner; r4-2 (TAMESHI-R4-2) adds
# --regression, this tool's own kill-rate regression gate (contract below);
# r6 (TAMESHI-T6-4) hardens --selftest with the r6 meta-sweep's surviving
# comparator mutants (corpus/knowledge/self-hardening-r6.md); r7-1
# (TAMESHI-T7-1) turns --regression into the fleet leg ledger — every recorded
# mutation case replayed at its pinned original revision, materialized from its
# repo's git object store (`git show <rev>:<path>`, immune to working-tree
# drift), with each leg's recorded kill-rate as its expectation; r10-2
# (TAMESHI-T10-2) resolves the round-8 leg cut — cases 5/6 (kagi
# `corpus/checklist-run.sh`, kumitate `scripts/claims-check.mjs`) embedded as
# the fifth and sixth legs; r16 (TAMESHI-R16) adds the seventh measured gate,
# kumitate scripts/round-gate.mjs, as js-round-gate; r18 adds the eighth measured
# leg, kumitate scripts/sources-check.mjs, as js-sources-check.
#
# Plan anchors: r3-2 = plans/round-3.plan.md:6-7 — "the method ships as tameshi's
# own runnable tool (tools/mutate-and-run.sh: inject->run->restore with
# byte-identity proof)"; r4-2 = plans/round-4.plan.md:9-10 — "mutate-and-run.sh
# gains a --regression mode (re-run both cases as a kill-rate regression gate for
# the tool itself)"; r10-2 = the round-8/9 named hand-offs — the leg cut stands
# with the tool owner (fleet-kill-rate-ledger.md ER-2 named follow-ups;
# mutation-case-kagi-checklist.md U5; mutation-case-kumitate-claims.md E3;
# round-9 record draft: "cases 5/6 remain unembedded — named debt") and is
# resolved here as an embedding, not a re-cut. Method provenance:
# corpus/knowledge/mutation-case-kimi-census.md §0-§1 — every mutant is a fresh
# copy of a pinned pristine; one exact edit per copy with a uniqueness assert; a
# green control run; killed/survived recorded per defect; sha256 before/after as
# the restore proof; a kill-rate headline.
#
# Inputs: a target file, a defect spec file, and a suite command. For each
# defect: if this one exact edit were present in the target, would the suite go
# red? Per-defect verdicts: KILLED (suite exit != 0) | SURVIVED (suite exit 0) |
# INCONCLUSIVE (timeout 124, or 126/127 = suite not runnable). The target is
# never written: every mutant is a fresh byte copy of the pinned pristine copy,
# and the target's sha256 before/after is the restore proof.
#
# Defect spec format v1 (one directive per line; '#' comments and blank lines
# ignored; LF endings only):
#
#   @defect <id> | <description>     id: [A-Za-z0-9._-]+; description optional
#   @old <text>                      exact string to find (required)
#   @new <text>                      exact replacement (may be empty to delete)
#
#   Escapes inside <text>: \n newline, \t tab, \r CR, \\ backslash. Any other
#   backslash sequence is refused (fail-closed against spec mistakes). Exactly
#   one separator space follows the directive; write a literal leading space in
#   the text as a second space (i.e. `@old ` + the text, spaces preserved).
#   The old string must occur exactly once (asserted before any suite run);
#   @new == @old (a no-op edit) is refused.
#
# Suite command contract: --suite CMD is run as `bash -c CMD` with stdin
# </dev/null, once per run, from the caller's CWD, with these exported:
#   MUTANT    absolute path of the artifact under test (the pristine copy for
#             the control run)          TARGET   absolute path of the original
#   PRISTINE  pinned pristine copy      WORKDIR  evidence/work directory
#   DEFECT_ID  defect id (CONTROL for the control run)
# Exit-code semantics: 0 = suite green; any non-zero = red, except
# 124 (timeout) / 126 / 127 (suite not runnable) = INCONCLUSIVE.
# Example: --suite 'node "$MUTANT" --selftest'
#
# Order of operations (fail-closed): (1) copy the target to a pinned pristine
# and pin both hashes; (2) parse the spec; (3) build every mutant and assert
# count==1 — any failure exits 2 before a single suite run; (4) control run on
# the pristine — a red control exits 2 (kills could not be attributed); (5)
# mutant runs with output tails; (6) re-check target+pristine sha256; (7)
# kill-rate report. A low kill count is a finding for the report, not a failure
# of this tool (bands are a report convention, not a gate — SKILL.md:127-133).
#
# Verdict lines (the last non-empty line is the verdict):
#   MUTATE_TOOL / MUTATE_DATE / MUTATE_TARGET / MUTATE_SPEC   — provenance
#   CONTROL: GREEN|RED (exit=… duration-ms=… pristine-sha256=…)
#   DEFECT <id>: KILLED|SURVIVED|INCONCLUSIVE (exit=… duration-ms=…)
#   MUTATE_RESTORE: OK|DRIFT (target-sha256 before=… after=… …)
#   MUTATE: DONE|DEGRADED (target=… defects=… killed=… survived=… inconclusive=… kill-rate=…%)
#   MUTATE: SETUP (<reason>)   — refused before/instead of a sweep
#
# Exit map (run): 0 done · 1 done-with-inconclusive (DEGRADED) · 2 setup/refusal ·
# 64 usage error.
#
# --regression (r4-2; r7-1 leg ledger; r10-2 six legs; r16 seven; r18 eight) — the fleet's
# mutation cases, replayed as legs. Each leg carries its recorded revision pin
# and its recorded verdict table; the recorded kill-rate is the leg's
# expectation:
#   js-kimi-census        kumitate-shokunin/scripts/kimi-census.mjs   8/10 (80 %)
#   bash-surface-census   kagi-shokunin/corpus/surface-census.sh      8/10 (80 %)
#   bash-tsukuroi-census  tsukuroi-shokunin/tools/tsukuroi-census.sh  6/10 (60 %)
#   bash-mado-verify      mado-shokunin/tools/mado-verify.sh          4/10 (40 %)
#   bash-checklist-run    kagi-shokunin/corpus/checklist-run.sh       7/15 (47 %)
#   js-claims-check       kumitate-shokunin/scripts/claims-check.mjs  7/10 (70 %)
#   js-round-gate         kumitate-shokunin/scripts/round-gate.mjs    5/10 (50 %)
#   js-sources-check      kumitate-shokunin/scripts/sources-check.mjs 5/10 (50 %)
# Sources: corpus/knowledge/mutation-case-kimi-census.md §1,
# mutation-case-kagi-census.md §1, mutation-case-tsukuroi-census.md §1,
# mutation-case-mado-verify.md §1, mutation-case-kagi-checklist.md §2,
# mutation-case-kumitate-claims.md §1, mutation-case-kumitate-round-gate.md §1,
# .ufo/evidence/round-18/sources-check/sources-check.defects.spec (leg 8).
# Leg 8's spec is the round-18 pre-registration committed in 93d22aa before
# any mutant ran; its table is the measured 2026-10-20 sweep (19:41Z,
# .ufo/evidence/round-18/sources-check/sweep/sweep.txt; 10/10 predictions held).
# All eight defect specs are embedded below (legs 1–4 cut from those notes'
# §7/§8/§9 reproduce manifests, all 40 @old strings re-verified count==1
# against the pinned bytes 2026-10-14; legs 5–6 are the recorded T8-1/T8-2
# spec files, their 25 @old strings re-verified count==1 against the pinned
# blobs at r10-2; leg 7's spec is the pre-registered round-16 spec verbatim
# and its table is the measured 2026-10-20 sweep; leg 8 likewise: the round-18
# pre-registered spec verbatim and its measured sweep table).
# r7-1: the target is NOT read from the fleet working tree. Each leg is
# materialized from its repo's git object store — `git show <rev>:<path>` — so
# a working tree that has drifted past the recorded revision (tsukuroi and
# mado: their pins are ancestors of HEAD) or carries uncommitted edits cannot
# move a replay. The materialized copy's sha256 must equal the recorded pin;
# a missing repo/rev/blob or a pin mismatch is a SETUP refusal, never a pass.
# r10-2 mirrors the blob's mode too (`git show` writes bytes, not modes): a
# gate whose selftest re-execs itself (checklist-run.sh runs "$SELF") needs the
# recorded exec bit, so 100755 blobs are chmod +x'd after materialization.
# Each leg then runs as a full CLI sub-invocation of this tool over the
# materialized copy; the fleet trees are never written.
# Stated expectation: ledger legs are byte-pinned replays, so their expectation
# is exact — 0 verdict flips (every measured row must equal the recorded
# table), killed-delta 0, defects exactly the recorded count, inconclusive 0,
# control green, MUTATE_RESTORE: OK. The r4-2 tolerance (|killed-recorded| <= 1
# and at most 1 verdict flip) remains the default for fixture-mode cases and is
# exercised by --selftest. Inside the stated expectation is PASS; outside it is
# FAIL. Exit map (--regression): 0 all cases PASS · 1 >= 1 case FAIL (measured
# table outside its expectation) · 2 a case could not be verified — missing
# repo/rev/blob, pin mismatch, sweep setup refusal — with no FAIL. Lines:
# REGRESSION_TOOL / REGRESSION_DATE / REGRESSION_BAND / REGRESSION_EXPECT /
# REGRESSION_PIN (one per leg: git source + pinned target sha256; the sweep's
# own MUTATE_TOOL line carries the tool pin) / REGRESSION_SWEEP /
# REGRESSION_TABLE / REGRESSION_DEFECT (one per differing row) / REGRESSION_CASE
# / REGRESSION_WORKDIR / REGRESSION: PASS|FAIL|SETUP. Scratch root: mktemp -d
# /tmp/tameshi-r4-2-regression.XXXXXX by default; removed on PASS unless --keep,
# kept on FAIL/SETUP, never removed when --workdir was given. In this mode
# --target/--defects/--suite/--label are refused (usage error).
#
# Evidence: workdir keeps pristine copy, mutant files, evidence/<id>.out full
# logs, evidence/control.out, defects.tsv (id/line/desc), results.tsv (per
# defect: verdict/exit/duration/mutant-sha256/desc), spec.copy; the path is
# printed as MUTATE_EVIDENCE. Default: mktemp -d /tmp/tameshi-r4-2-run.XXXXXX.
#
# --selftest is hermetic: it builds a tiny fixture target + mini-suites + four
# fixture specs under /tmp/tameshi-r4-2-selftest.XXXXXX and self-checks
# control-green, one kill, one survivor, exact kill-rate line, restore proof,
# DEGRADED path, refusal paths (duplicate old string, unknown escape, no-op
# edit, missing --target, usage), and the --regression engine on the same
# fixture (in-band PASS, band-edge PASS at killed-delta 1 / flips 1, doctored
# table FAIL, an unmeasured recorded entry FAIL, an unrecorded measured row
# FAIL, a DEGRADED sub-sweep FAIL on inconclusive=1, a fully-matching all-killed
# table PASS, pin mismatch and missing target SETUP; r7-1 adds, over a
# throwaway git repo, the ledger engine: git-materialized replay PASS,
# drift-immune replay of the pinned blob, blob/pin mismatch and unresolvable
# revision SETUP, and the exact-tolerance arm where one flipped recorded entry
# FAILs; r10-2 adds the embedded-ledger sweep (r16 extends it to seven legs;
# r18 extends it to eight): the leg specs are written and parsed, and each recorded
# table's ids, total and killed count are checked
# against its own spec, plus the exec-bit mirror arms (a blob committed 100755
# runs a self-exec suite; the same bytes committed 100644 are refused at their
# red control) — hermetic, no fleet path read). Scratch is removed on PASS,
# kept on FAIL for inspection.
#
# Dependencies: bash, python3 (byte-exact edit + spec parse), git (--regression
# materialization + the selftest's ledger-engine cases), coreutils (sha256sum,
# mktemp, timeout, cp, date).

set -u -o pipefail

VERSION='mutate-and-run.sh r10-2 (tameshi-shokunin, 2026-10-14)'
SELF=$(readlink -f "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")

MODE=run
TARGET=''
SPEC=''
SUITE=''
WORKDIR=''
LABEL=''
TIMEOUT_S=300
TAIL_N=8
BAND_KILLED=1   # --regression: |killed - recorded| tolerance, per case
BAND_FLIPS=1    # --regression: max verdict differences vs the recorded table
KEEP=0          # --regression: keep the scratch root also on PASS

usage() {
  cat <<'EOF'
mutate-and-run.sh — generic mutation runner (tameshi-shokunin)

Usage:
  mutate-and-run.sh --target FILE --defects SPEC --suite CMD [options]
  mutate-and-run.sh --regression [--workdir DIR] [--keep]
  mutate-and-run.sh --selftest
  mutate-and-run.sh --help

Inputs:
  --target FILE    artifact to mutate (never written; sha256 pinned before/after)
  --defects SPEC   defect spec file (format below)
  --suite CMD      suite command, run as `bash -c CMD` with $MUTANT set to the
                   artifact under test (the pristine copy for the control run)

Options:
  --workdir DIR    evidence/work dir (default: mktemp -d /tmp/tameshi-r4-2-run.XXXXXX)
  --label NAME     name for the report (default: basename of the target)
  --timeout N      seconds per suite run (default: 300)
  --tail N         output tail lines shown per run (default: 8)
  --keep           --regression only: keep the scratch root also on PASS

--regression replays every recorded mutation case as a leg:
(js-kimi-census, bash-surface-census, bash-tsukuroi-census, bash-mado-verify,
bash-checklist-run, js-claims-check, js-round-gate, js-sources-check) at its pinned
revision, materialized from the repo's git object store (`git show
<rev>:<path>` — immune to working-tree drift; a missing repo/rev/blob or a pin
mismatch is a SETUP refusal). Each leg must reproduce its recorded verdict table exactly:
the recorded kill-rate is the leg's expectation (flips 0, killed-delta 0,
count exactly the recorded defect count, inconclusive 0, control green, restore
OK). Exit: 0 all legs PASS · 1 >= 1 leg out of band · 2 cannot verify (missing
repo/rev/blob / pin mismatch / setup refusal). Fleet trees are never written.
See the file header for the full contract and evidence layout.

Spec format v1 (one directive per line; '#' comments; blank lines ignored):
  @defect <id> | <description>
  @old <text>          exact string to find (count==1 asserted before any run)
  @new <text>          exact replacement (may be empty)
  escapes: \n \t \r \\; any other backslash sequence is refused.

Verdicts per defect: KILLED | SURVIVED | INCONCLUSIVE. Exit map (run): 0 done ·
1 done-with-inconclusive · 2 setup/refusal · 64 usage.
EOF
}

usage_exit() {
  printf 'mutate-and-run: %s\n' "$1" >&2
  usage >&2
  exit 64
}

die_setup() {
  printf 'MUTATE: SETUP (%s)\n' "$1" >&2
  exit 2
}

need_val() {
  if [[ $# -lt 2 || -z "${2-}" ]]; then
    usage_exit "$1 needs a value"
  fi
}

sha_of() {
  local h
  h=$(sha256sum -- "$1" | cut -d' ' -f1) || die_setup "sha256sum failed for $1"
  printf '%s' "$h"
}

# r17 (TAMESHI-R17) GIT_* scrub (N01 F1/F14; R04's 12:29 lane clobber). Every
# git call in this tool goes through git_clean: a caller-exported GIT_DIR /
# GIT_WORK_TREE / GIT_INDEX_FILE (git hooks, `git rebase -x`, editor or jj
# integrations) otherwise redirects `git -C <fixture>` writes into the caller's
# repository. R04 ran `GIT_DIR=<lane>/.git bash tools/mutate-and-run.sh
# --selftest` and the fixture `init/add/commit` sequence committed into the live
# lane and rewrote its index. The unset list is derived from the live
# environment, so every GIT_* name is scrubbed, including ones not spelled out
# here (GIT_CONFIG_*, GIT_AUTHOR_*, GIT_TRACE*, …).
git_clean() {
  local -a scrub=()
  local name
  while IFS= read -r name; do
    [[ -n "$name" ]] && scrub+=(-u "$name")
  done < <(env | sed -n 's/^\(GIT_[A-Za-z0-9_]*\)=.*/\1/p')
  if [[ ${#scrub[@]} -gt 0 ]]; then
    env "${scrub[@]}" git "$@"
  else
    git "$@"
  fi
}

# fixture_commit <repo> <message> [<path>...] — the selftest's fixture history
# builder: init (idempotent), stage everything (or the named paths), commit with
# the fixture identity, print the commit id. All steps go through git_clean, so
# the selftest's canary arm can re-run this exact step under an exported GIT_DIR
# and prove it cannot land outside <repo>.
fixture_commit() {
  local r=$1 msg=$2
  shift 2
  git_clean -C "$r" init -q || return 1
  if [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; then
    git_clean -C "$r" add -- "$@" || return 1
  else
    git_clean -C "$r" add -A || return 1
  fi
  git_clean -C "$r" -c user.name=tameshi -c user.email=tameshi@selftest -c commit.gpgsign=false \
    commit -q -m "$msg" || return 1
  git_clean -C "$r" rev-parse HEAD
}

print_tail() {
  local f=$1 n
  n=$(wc -l < "$f")
  if [[ "$n" -eq 0 ]]; then
    printf '  | (no output)\n'
    return 0
  fi
  while IFS= read -r l; do printf '  | %s\n' "$l"; done < <(tail -n "$TAIL_N" "$f")
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- spec helper

py_helper() {
  python3 - "$@" <<'PYEOF'
"""Spec helper for mutate-and-run.sh: `list <spec>` | `apply <spec> <pristine>
<mutant> <id>`. Byte-exact single replace; count==1 asserted; refuses no-ops."""
import sys

ESCAPES = {'n': '\n', 't': '\t', 'r': '\r', '\\': '\\'}


def die(msg):
    sys.stderr.write('spec: %s\n' % msg)
    sys.exit(2)


def unescape(s, where):
    out = []
    i = 0
    while i < len(s):
        c = s[i]
        if c == '\\':
            if i + 1 >= len(s):
                die('%s: trailing backslash' % where)
            n = s[i + 1]
            if n not in ESCAPES:
                die("%s: unknown escape '\\%s' (supported: \\n \\t \\r \\\\)" % (where, n))
            out.append(ESCAPES[n])
            i += 2
        else:
            out.append(c)
            i += 1
    return ''.join(out)


def parse(path):
    try:
        with open(path, encoding='utf-8') as fh:
            text = fh.read()
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as e:
        die('cannot read spec file %s: %s' % (path, e))
    if '\r' in text:
        die('CR characters in spec (CRLF line endings are not supported)')
    defects = []
    cur = None

    def finish():
        nonlocal cur
        if cur is not None and ('old' not in cur or 'new' not in cur):
            die('line %d: defect %s incomplete (needs both @old and @new)' % (cur['line'], cur['id']))
        cur = None

    for no, line in enumerate(text.split('\n'), 1):
        if line.strip() == '':
            continue
        if line.lstrip().startswith('#'):
            continue
        if line == '@defect' or line.startswith('@defect '):
            finish()
            rest = line[len('@defect'):].lstrip(' ')
            idpart, sep, desc = rest.partition('|')
            idpart, desc = idpart.strip(), desc.strip()
            if not idpart or any(ch not in 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789._-' for ch in idpart):
                die('line %d: bad or missing defect id: %r' % (no, idpart))
            if any(d['id'] == idpart for d in defects):
                die('line %d: duplicate defect id %s' % (no, idpart))
            if '\t' in desc:
                die('line %d: description must not contain a tab' % no)
            cur = {'id': idpart, 'desc': desc, 'line': no}
            defects.append(cur)
        elif line == '@old' or line.startswith('@old '):
            if cur is None:
                die('line %d: @old outside a defect' % no)
            if 'old' in cur:
                die('line %d: duplicate @old for defect %s' % (no, cur['id']))
            cur['old'] = unescape('' if line == '@old' else line[len('@old '):], 'line %d' % no)
        elif line == '@new' or line.startswith('@new '):
            if cur is None:
                die('line %d: @new outside a defect' % no)
            if 'new' in cur:
                die('line %d: duplicate @new for defect %s' % (no, cur['id']))
            cur['new'] = unescape('' if line == '@new' else line[len('@new '):], 'line %d' % no)
        else:
            die('line %d: unrecognized line: %r' % (no, line[:80]))
    finish()
    if not defects:
        die('spec contains no defects')
    for d in defects:
        if d['old'] == '':
            die('defect %s: @old text is empty' % d['id'])
        if d['old'] == d['new']:
            die('defect %s: @new equals @old (no-op mutation)' % d['id'])
    return defects


def cmd_list(path):
    for d in parse(path):
        sys.stdout.write('%s\t%d\t%s\n' % (d['id'], d['line'], d['desc']))


def cmd_apply(path, pristine, mutant, did):
    hits = [d for d in parse(path) if d['id'] == did]
    if not hits:
        die('defect %s not found in spec' % did)
    d = hits[0]
    try:
        data = open(pristine, 'rb').read()
    except OSError as e:
        die('cannot read pristine %s: %s' % (pristine, e))
    old = d['old'].encode('utf-8')
    new = d['new'].encode('utf-8')
    count = data.count(old)
    if count != 1:
        sys.stderr.write('APPLY FAIL id=%s line=%d old-string-count=%d expected=1\n' % (did, d['line'], count))
        sys.exit(2)
    result = data.replace(old, new, 1)
    try:
        with open(mutant, 'wb') as fh:
            fh.write(result)
    except OSError as e:
        die('cannot write mutant %s: %s' % (mutant, e))
    sys.stdout.write('APPLY OK id=%s old-count=1 bytes-before=%d bytes-after=%d\n' % (did, len(data), len(result)))


if len(sys.argv) < 2 or sys.argv[1] not in ('list', 'apply'):
    sys.stderr.write('helper: usage: list <spec> | apply <spec> <pristine> <mutant> <id>\n')
    sys.exit(2)
if sys.argv[1] == 'list':
    if len(sys.argv) != 3:
        die('helper: list needs <spec>')
    cmd_list(sys.argv[2])
else:
    if len(sys.argv) != 6:
        die('helper: apply needs <spec> <pristine> <mutant> <id>')
    cmd_apply(sys.argv[2], sys.argv[3], sys.argv[4], sys.argv[5])
PYEOF
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- sweep

run_one() { # $1 = artifact under test, $2 = evidence file, $3 = defect id
  local start now
  start=$(date +%s%N)
  MUTANT="$1" TARGET="$TARGET_ABS" PRISTINE="$PRISTINE" WORKDIR="$WORKDIR" DEFECT_ID="$3" \
    timeout "$TIMEOUT_S" bash -c "$SUITE" </dev/null >"$2" 2>&1
  RC=$?
  now=$(date +%s%N)
  DUR_MS=$(( (now - start) / 1000000 ))
}

run_sweep() {
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || die_setup 'python3 missing'
  command -v sha256sum >/dev/null 2>&1 || die_setup 'sha256sum missing'
  command -v timeout >/dev/null 2>&1 || die_setup 'timeout missing'
  command -v mktemp >/dev/null 2>&1 || die_setup 'mktemp missing'

  [[ -n "$TARGET" ]] || usage_exit 'missing --target'
  [[ -n "$SPEC" ]] || usage_exit 'missing --defects'
  [[ -n "$SUITE" ]] || usage_exit 'missing --suite'
  [[ -f "$TARGET" ]] || die_setup "target is not a regular file: $TARGET"
  [[ -r "$TARGET" ]] || die_setup "target is not readable: $TARGET"
  [[ -f "$SPEC" ]] || die_setup "spec is not a regular file: $SPEC"
  [[ -r "$SPEC" ]] || die_setup "spec is not readable: $SPEC"
  (( TIMEOUT_S > 0 )) || usage_exit '--timeout must be a positive integer'
  (( TAIL_N > 0 )) || usage_exit '--tail must be a positive integer'

  TARGET_ABS=$(readlink -f "$TARGET")
  SPEC_ABS=$(readlink -f "$SPEC")

  if [[ -z "$WORKDIR" ]]; then
    WORKDIR=$(mktemp -d /tmp/tameshi-r4-2-run.XXXXXX) || die_setup 'mktemp -d failed'
  else
    mkdir -p "$WORKDIR" || die_setup "cannot create workdir: $WORKDIR"
  fi
  EVID="$WORKDIR/evidence"
  mkdir -p "$EVID" || die_setup "cannot create evidence dir: $EVID"
  [[ -n "$LABEL" ]] || LABEL=$(basename "$TARGET_ABS")

  TARGET_SHA0=$(sha_of "$TARGET_ABS")
  PRISTINE="$WORKDIR/pristine.$(basename "$TARGET_ABS")"
  cp -p "$TARGET_ABS" "$PRISTINE" || die_setup "cannot copy target to pristine: $PRISTINE"
  PRISTINE_SHA=$(sha_of "$PRISTINE")
  PRISTINE_BYTES=$(wc -c < "$PRISTINE")
  [[ "$TARGET_SHA0" == "$PRISTINE_SHA" ]] || die_setup 'read race: target changed while copying'

  printf 'MUTATE_TOOL: %s sha256=%s\n' "$SELF" "$(sha_of "$SELF")"
  printf 'MUTATE_DATE: %s\n' "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
  printf 'MUTATE_TARGET: %s sha256=%s bytes=%s\n' "$TARGET_ABS" "$TARGET_SHA0" "$PRISTINE_BYTES"

  local out
  if ! out=$(py_helper list "$SPEC_ABS" 2>&1); then
    printf '%s\n' "$out"
    die_setup 'spec parse failed'
  fi
  local total
  total=$(printf '%s\n' "$out" | wc -l)
  printf 'MUTATE_SPEC: %s defects=%s\n' "$SPEC_ABS" "$total"
  printf '%s\n' "$out" > "$WORKDIR/defects.tsv"
  [[ "$SPEC_ABS" == "$WORKDIR/spec.copy" ]] || cp -p "$SPEC_ABS" "$WORKDIR/spec.copy"

  local id line desc mutant aout fail=0
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r id line desc; do
    [[ -n "$id" ]] || continue
    mutant="$WORKDIR/mutant-$id.$(basename "$TARGET_ABS")"
    cp -p "$PRISTINE" "$mutant" || die_setup "cannot create mutant copy: $mutant"
    if ! aout=$(py_helper apply "$SPEC_ABS" "$PRISTINE" "$mutant" "$id" 2>&1); then
      printf '%s\n' "$aout"
      fail=$((fail + 1))
    fi
  done <<<"$out"
  if [[ "$fail" -gt 0 ]]; then
    die_setup "spec/preparation failed for $fail defect(s); no suite run"
  fi

  local ctl_rc ctl_ms
  run_one "$PRISTINE" "$EVID/control.out" CONTROL
  ctl_rc=$RC
  ctl_ms=$DUR_MS
  if [[ "$ctl_rc" -ne 0 ]]; then
    printf 'CONTROL: RED (exit=%s duration-ms=%s pristine-sha256=%s)\n' "$ctl_rc" "$ctl_ms" "$PRISTINE_SHA"
    print_tail "$EVID/control.out"
    die_setup "control run on the pristine copy is red (exit=$ctl_rc); cannot attribute kills; log: $EVID/control.out"
  fi
  printf 'CONTROL: GREEN (exit=0 duration-ms=%s pristine-sha256=%s)\n' "$ctl_ms" "$PRISTINE_SHA"
  print_tail "$EVID/control.out"

  local killed=0 survived=0 inconcl=0 v note
  printf 'id\tverdict\texit\tduration-ms\tmutant-sha256\tdesc\n' > "$WORKDIR/results.tsv"
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r id line desc; do
    [[ -n "$id" ]] || continue
    mutant="$WORKDIR/mutant-$id.$(basename "$TARGET_ABS")"
    run_one "$mutant" "$EVID/$id.out" "$id"
    note=''
    case $RC in
      0) v=SURVIVED; survived=$((survived + 1)) ;;
      124) v=INCONCLUSIVE; inconcl=$((inconcl + 1)); note=' note=timeout' ;;
      126) v=INCONCLUSIVE; inconcl=$((inconcl + 1)); note=' note=suite-not-executable' ;;
      127) v=INCONCLUSIVE; inconcl=$((inconcl + 1)); note=' note=suite-not-found' ;;
      *) v=KILLED; killed=$((killed + 1)) ;;
    esac
    printf 'DEFECT %s: %s (exit=%s%s duration-ms=%s) %s\n' "$id" "$v" "$RC" "$note" "$DUR_MS" "$desc"
    print_tail "$EVID/$id.out"
    printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$id" "$v" "$RC" "$DUR_MS" "$(sha_of "$mutant")" "$desc" >> "$WORKDIR/results.tsv"
  done <<<"$out"

  local target_sha1 pristine_sha1
  target_sha1=$(sha_of "$TARGET_ABS")
  pristine_sha1=$(sha_of "$PRISTINE")
  if [[ "$TARGET_SHA0" != "$target_sha1" || "$PRISTINE_SHA" != "$pristine_sha1" ]]; then
    printf 'MUTATE_RESTORE: DRIFT (target-sha256 before=%s after=%s pristine-sha256-before=%s after=%s)\n' \
      "$TARGET_SHA0" "$target_sha1" "$PRISTINE_SHA" "$pristine_sha1"
    die_setup 'byte-identity proof failed: target or pristine changed during the sweep'
  fi
  printf 'MUTATE_RESTORE: OK (target-sha256 before=%s after=%s pristine-sha256=%s)\n' \
    "$TARGET_SHA0" "$target_sha1" "$pristine_sha1"
  printf 'MUTATE_EVIDENCE: %s\n' "$WORKDIR"

  local pct
  pct=$(( (200 * killed + total) / (2 * total) ))
  if [[ "$inconcl" -gt 0 ]]; then
    printf 'MUTATE: DEGRADED (target=%s defects=%s killed=%s survived=%s inconclusive=%s kill-rate=%s%%)\n' \
      "$LABEL" "$total" "$killed" "$survived" "$inconcl" "$pct"
    exit 1
  fi
  printf 'MUTATE: DONE (target=%s defects=%s killed=%s survived=%s inconclusive=%s kill-rate=%s%%)\n' \
    "$LABEL" "$total" "$killed" "$survived" "$inconcl" "$pct"
  exit 0
}

# --------------------------------------------------------------- regression

# Recorded expectations for --regression (r7-1 leg ledger; r10-2 leg cut
# resolved — cases 5/6 embedded). Anchors:
# corpus/knowledge/mutation-case-kimi-census.md §1 (js — control green, killed
# D1,D3-D9, survived D2,D10, kill-rate 80 % at :29),
# mutation-case-kagi-census.md §1 (bash — control green, killed B1-B4,B6-B9,
# survived B5,B10, kill-rate 80 % at :50-53),
# mutation-case-tsukuroi-census.md §1 (bash — control green, killed B1-B6,
# survived B7-B10, kill-rate 60 % at :69-70),
# mutation-case-mado-verify.md §1 (bash — control green, killed D1,D2,D4,D6,
# survived D3,D5,D7-D9,R1, kill-rate 40 % at :61-62),
# mutation-case-kagi-checklist.md §2 (bash — control green, killed A2,B2,B4,C2,
# D2,E1,F1, survived A1,B1,B3,C1,D1,E2,F2,G1, 7/15 = 47 % at :130-131),
# mutation-case-kumitate-claims.md §1 (js — control green, killed D1-D6,D10,
# survived D7,D8,D9, 7/10 = 70 % at :63-65). The specs below: legs 1–4 are
# those notes' §7/§8/§9 reproduce manifests (re-verified count==1 against the
# pinned blobs on 2026-10-14); legs 5–6 are the recorded T8-1/T8-2 spec files
# (/tmp/w8-tameshi-t81/checklist.spec; /tmp/w8-t8-2-claims/spec/
# claims-check.defects.spec), re-verified count==1 against the pinned blobs at
# r10-2; leg 7 (js-round-gate) was measured in round 16 — its spec is the
# pre-registered spec verbatim, its table the measured sweep. REV_* = the git revision each leg's bytes are
# materialized from
# (`git show <rev>:<repo path>`).
PIN_JS=83016d45049b009a6c9868dc392ff6ebcecb357c9abfe423f476459f2724f426
